# Faces

The print theme names one family for everything, so the sans stack and the
monospace stack are the only two a document can reach without embedding a font
of its own. Four faces come out of the first stack and the styles applied to
it, and the code font is the second.

Body text is regular. **Bold is a different face, not a heavier stroke.**
*Italic is oblique, because the standard 14 has no true italic for a sans
face.* ***Both at once resolves to one face rather than two passes.***

`Inline code` is monospace, and **`bold inline code`** is the bold cut of it.
A heading is bold, which is why the title above is not the face this sentence
is set in.

```ts
const face = 'Courier';
	// A tab is four spaces wide before it reaches the encoder.
```

## What WinAnsi cannot hold

A codepoint outside the encoding is drawn as `?` and reported once, rather
than silently dropped: 文 is not a character this font can encode.
